  "answer": "To address the credit-assignment problem where long-delayed effects make it difficult for a learner to attribute outcomes to specific past actions, it has been proposed to enrich rule-sets with \"silly rules,\" such as arbitrary taboos against harmless actions (https://arxiv.org/abs/2001.09318). \n\nThe mechanism relies on social rules enforced by third-party punishment (https://arxiv.org/abs/2001.09318). Because these norms inherently encode information about causal relationships, they relieve individual agents from having to discover and understand the long-delayed effects on their own; instead, the agents only need to follow the rules and are incentivized to do so to avoid immediate punishment (https://arxiv.org/abs/2001.09318)."
